[;ray_intersects_triangle
 [High-level description
  So there's this ray described by the tuple, { ray = (ray_P, ray_dir) }
  and a triangle described by { triangle = (O, A, B) }
  NOTE We make everything be relative to one corner of the triangle.
  { O = [0,0,0] }
  Because things are simpler that way.
  And it lets us talk about {A} and {B} as vectors.
  The question is, when does the ray hit the triangle?
  
  Let's say the ray hits the triangle at the point {Q} (note if it ever does, it may not)
  {
   Q = ray_P + t ray_dir
  }
  
  {Q} can also be written as { Q = u A + v B }
  
  // {Q} is in the triangle when {u,v >= 0 and u+v <= 1}
  
  // Let's do the move when you combine the equations
  ray_P + t ray_dir = u A + v B
  // NOTE We can figure out {t} from "ray_intersects_plane",
  // but we're not gonna use it.
  
  // The main insight is to rewrite it like this.
  u A + v B + t (-ray_dir) = ray_P
  // "A", "B" and "ray_dir" together form a coordinate system,
  // once you can expression "ray_P" in that coordinate system,
  // you can just read off "u" and "v".
  
  // Well, you might think just inverting this matrix
  [A B -ray_dir]
  // and we'd be good to go, but here's a thing:
  // We only need to solve for one solution "ray_P".
  // Inverting the matrix would mean solving for all possible solutions.
  // @cramers_rule is more efficient for this (probably).
  ]
 
 There's this (non-vetted) code from
 http://www.lighthouse3d.com/tutorials/maths/ray-triangle-intersection/ 
 Source: Moller/Trumbore https://www.graphics.cornell.edu/pubs/1997/MT97.pdf
 {
  function b32
   ray_intersects_triangle(v3 ray_P, v3 ray_dir,
                           v3 O, v3 A, v3 B)
  {
   A -= O;
   B -= O;
   
   v3 h = cross(ray_dir, B);
   v1 det = dot(A, h);
   
   if(absolute(det) < 0.00001)
   {// NOTE(kv) This happens when "h" is perpendicular to "A",
    // which happens when "ray_dir" is perpendicular to the
    // normal of the plane containing the triangle "(O,A,B)".
    return(false);
   }
   else
   {
    ray_P -= O;
    v1 det_inv = 1.f / det;
    v1 u = det_inv * dot(ray_P, h);
    
    if(u < 0.f or u > 1.f)
    {
     return(false);
    }
    else
    {
     v3 q = cross(ray_P,A);
     v1 v = det_inv * dot(ray_dir, q);
     
     if (v < 0.0 or u + v > 1.0)
     {
      return(false);
     }
     else
     {// NOTE At this stage we can compute "t" to find out where
      // the intersection point is on the line.
      t = det_inv * dot(B,q);
      
      if(t > 0.00001)  // ray intersection
      {
       return(true);
      }
      else
      {// NOTE This means that there is a line intersection
       // but not a ray intersection
       return (false);
      }
     }
    }
   }
  }
 }
]

[Apparently there is a reason why [barycentric combinations] make sense
 {C = sum(i in [0,n], a__i B__i)}
 where the {a__i} sum up 1
 because {C} can be rewritten as a point-vector addition
 {B__0 + sum(i in [1,n],  a__i (B__i - B__0))}
 because
 {
  B__0 + sum(i in [1,n],  a__i (B__i - B__0))
  // NOTE splitting out the sum
  = B__0 + sum(i in [1,n], a__i B__i) - sum(i in [1,n], a__i) B__0
  // modify the second sum
  = B__0 + sum(i in [1,n], a__i B__i) - sum(i in [0,n], a__i) B__0 + a__0 B__0
  // Hooray! We can simplify the second sum, and obtain our goal
  = sum(i in [0,n], a__i B__i)
 }
 
 [Well what kind of vector is {sum(i in [1,n],  a__i (B__i - B__0))}?
  the weights could also be zeros, in which case the vector is 0, and {C=B}
  or, one of the weights could be one,
  in which case the vector is {B[i] - B[0]}, and {C=B[i]}
  ]
]

[The ratio discussion (doesn't seem intresting?)
 What is a "ratio"? ratio is only defined for 3 collinear points
 According to the book:
 {ratio(a,b,c) = signed_distance(a,b) / signed_distance(b,c)}
 I have no idea how you compute {signed_distance}.
 But I'm guessing that it is like this
 {ratio(a,b,c) = (b-a) / (c-b)}
 [How are we supposed to do this divide? Well we can just take one dimension
  {ratio(a,b,c) = (bx-ax) / (cx-bx)}
  If {cx-bx = 0}, then choose to do it in {y}, and if all dimensions are zero,
  the ratio is undefined anyway]
 [NOTE So if {b} isn't in the middle of {a} and {c}, stuff is gonna be negative!]
 [NOTE If we swap the endpoints, the ratio will be inverted
  {ratio(c,b,a) = (b-c) / (a-b) = (c-a) / (b-a)
   = 1 / ratio(a,b,c)}]
 
 [#fact If {t} is the lerp coordinate of {b} with respect to {a,c}
  meaning {b = (1-t)a + t c}
  It follows that {ratio(a,b,c) = t / (1-t)}]
 ]

[;Menelaos_theorem @Menelaos_theorem_test
 We have these points defined by this blossom notation
 (which doesn't make sense yet, and the author totally failed to mention that)
 {b[0,t] = lerp(b0,t,b1)
  b[1,s] = lerp(b1,s,b2)
  // etc.
 }
 Then we have these two points
 {
  b[s,t] = lerp(b[0,s], t, b[1,s])
  b[t,s] = lerp(b[0,t], s, b[1,t])
 }
 Menelaos' theorem says that the two points above are equal
 
 Well, we could just verify mechanically that these match
 {
  lerp(b[0,s], t, b[1,s])
  = (1-t)(1-s)b0 + ((1-t)s + t(1-s))b1 + t s b2
 }
 {
  lerp(b[0,t], s, b[1,t])
  = (1-s)(1-t)b0 + ((1-s)t + s(1-t))b1 + s t b2
 }
 And they do, and that is an amazing fact!
 
 Try varying {s} from 0 to 1, watch how {b[s,t]} moves.
 ]

[Affine map
 [What is an affine map?
  An affine map is a map that preserves barycentric combinations.
  Meaning that if {x = w1 a1 +...+ wn an} and {w1+...+wn = 1}, then
  {Phi(x) = w1 Phi(a1) +...+ wn Phi(an)}]
 
 [[Affine maps from {R^3} to itself] of the form
  { Phi(x) = A x + v } are affine maps
  Proof: suppose that { sum(w__i = 1) }
  {
   // starting from Phi of an affine combination
   Phi(sum(w__i x__i))
   = (A sum(w__i x__i)) + v
   // A is linear
   = sum(w__i A x__i) + v
   // Moving "v" into the sum is possible
   = sum(w__i A x__i) + sum(w__i) v
   = sum(w__i A x__i + w__i v)
   = sum(w__i (A x__i + v))
   = sum(w__i Phi(x__i))
   // ending with an affine combination of Phi's
  }
  
  [bookmark todo How would we prove the inverse of this statement?
   Prompt for the chatbot
   
   In the book "Curves and Surfaces for CAGD", section 2.2, it says this
   Let us now be more specific. In a given coordinate system, a point x is represented by a coordinate triple, which we also denote by x. An affine map now takes on the familiar form
   { Phi(x) = Ax + v }
   where {A} is a 3x3 matrix and {v} is a vector from {R^3}
   
   But there is no proof for this statement, where can I find the proof?
   The inverse of that statement is proven right after, but the statement isn't proven.
   Which leads me to believe that either it is too hard to include there, or it's obvious and I missed it somehow?
   
   
   ]
  ]
 ]

[;lerp_3_1
 Claim: linear interpolation is an affine map of the real line into a straight line in {E^3}
 
 
 If we want to claim that the linear interpolation between {a,b} above is an affine map,
 what would we need to prove?
 Suppose that {t = w1 t1 +...+ wn tn; w1+...+wn= 1}, then we need to show that
 {lerp(a,t,b) = w1 lerp(a,t1,b) +...+ wn lerp(a,tn,b)}
 It can be proven by simple algebraic manipulation
 
 [We can show this ;lerp_t_thm
  {lerp(lerp(a,x,b), t, lerp(a,y,b))
   = lerp(a,lerp(x,t,y),b)}
  because
  {lerp(a,lerp(x,t,y),b)
   = lerp(a, (1-t)x + t y, b)
   = (1-t) lerp(a,x,b) + t lerp(a,y,b)
   = lerp(lerp(a,x,b), t, lerp(a,y,b))}
  ]
 
 A fun, non-obvious corollary is:
 {lerp(A, s t, B) = lerp(A, t, lerp(A, s, B))}
]

[;two_way_split Quick way to prove that something is an affine map
 Suppose we have the two_way_split {Phi(w1 t1 + w2 t2) = w1 Phi(t1) + t2 Phi(t2)}
 We can prove that {Phi} is an affine map
 [Suppose we have {w1+w2+w3 = 1}
  {Phi(w1 t1 + w2 t2 + w3 t3)
   = Phi(w1 t1 + (w2+w3)(w2/(w2+w3) t2 + w3/(w2+w3) t3))
   // applying two_way_split
   = w1 Phi(t1) + (w2+w3) Phi(w2/(w2+w3) t2 + w3/(w2+w3) t3)
   // applying two_way_split again
   = w1 Phi(t1) + (w2+w3) (w2/(w2+w3) Phi(t2) + w3/(w2+w3) Phi(t3))
   = w1 Phi(t1) +  w2 Phi(t2) + w3 Phi(t3) // #qed
  }
  ]
 ]

[Blossom
 Referencing the function {b[t1,t2]} from @Menelaos_theorem
 
 [;blossom_multiaffine
  If {t1 = alpha r + beta s}, where {alpha+beta = 1}
  Then we have this multiaffinity property:
  {b[t1,t2]
   = b[alpha r + beta s, t2]
   = alpha b[r,t2] + beta b[s,t2]}
  [Why? when you expand things out
   {b[alpha r + beta s, t2]
    = lerp(lerp(b0,t2,b1), (alpha r + beta s), lerp(b1,t2,b2))
    = lerp(*, (alpha r + beta s), *)
    = alpha lerp(*,r,*) + beta lerp(*,s,*)
   }
   Remember from @lerp_3_1 that {lerp} is an affine map, so yeah, that tracks]]
 
 [Blossoming with permutation
  [How do you really know that permutation works?
   ie {b[t] == b[permute(t)]}]
  [Let's solve that with an example,
   suppose we have a cubic de Casteljau blossom {b[r,s,t]}
   We can mutate it like so
   {b[r,s,t]
    = lerp(lerp(lerp(b0,r,b1),s,lerp(b1,r,b2)),
           t,
           lerp(lerp(b1,r,b2),s,lerp(b2,r,b3)))
    = lerp(lerp(b01[r], s, b11[r]),
           t,
           lerp(b11[r], s, b21[r]))
    // NOTE Invoking @Menelaos_theorem
    = lerp(lerp(b01[r], t, b11[r]),
           s,
           lerp(b11[r], t, b21[r]))
    = lerp(b02[r,t], s, b12[r,t])
    = b[r,t,s]
   }
   ]
  ]
 
 [Special blossom arguments
  [#done Writing control points in the de Casteljau process as blossoms
   Each control point {bi} in the curve {b} can be written as
   {bi = b[1 (repeated i times), 0 (repeated n-i times)]}
   
   [Why: We can write blossoming process in the triangle scheme as follows
    (take cubic case) Suppose there are two 1's
    {
     b0 b1 b2 b2
     b1 b2 b3
     b2 b3
     b3
     // This checks out!
     b2 = b[0,1,1]
    }
    ]
   ]
  
  [We can write intermediate control points as blossoms
   {
    b0 b[t,0,0] b[t,t,0] b[t,t,t]
    b1 b[t,0,1] b[t,t,1]
    b2 b[t,1,1]
    b3
   }
   [How it works: So we just lerp with {t} until we reach the desired column,
    then sprinkle in some 1's to select the row, then the rest is 0's]
   ]
  ]
]

[;bezier_subdivision Ok so we have a bezier curve {b},
 And we wanna obtain a subcurve {c} that works like this:
 {c(t) = b(lerp(a,t,b)) = b(u)}
 
 [Let's cheese it out a little and look at the results,
  suppose we have a quadratic curve,
  the answer would be this (name collision alert!)
  {b[a,a], b[a,b], b[b,b]}
  [note A way to remember this would be:
   write the regular control points as blossoms {b[0,0], b[0,1], b[1,1]},
   then convert {0,1} to {a,b}]
  ]
 
 [#done Let's do a cubic case just to be sure
  {c = [b[a,a,a], b[a,a,b], b[a,b,b], b[b,b,b]]}
  [Detour
   NOTE Expressed in the language of the blossom
   {b[a,a,a] = lerp(b[a,a,0], a, b[a,a,1])}
   Therefore, you can just "factor out" the common blossom values
   {
    lerp(b[a,a,a], t, b[a,a,b])
    = lerp(lerp(b[a,a,0], a, b[a,a,1]),
           t,
           lerp(b[a,a,0], b, b[a,a,1]))
    = lerp(b[a,a,0], lerp(a,t,b), b[a,a,1])
    = b[a,a,u]
   }
   ]
  {
   c(t) = sample([b[a,a,a], b[a,a,b], b[a,b,b], b[b,b,b]], t)
   //NOTE Invoking de Casteljau's pyramid
   = sample([b[a,a,u], b[a,b,u], b[b,b,u]], t)
   = sample([b[a,u,u], b[b,u,u]], t)
   = b[u,u,u] // #qed
  }
  ]
]

[;binomial_coefficients
 {binom(n,i) = n C i = ?}
 Ground rule: normal cases are {0 <= i <= n}
 We know {n C 1 = n}
 Well what about 2 {n C 2 = (n C 1)(n-1 C 1) / 2 = n(n-1) / 2}
 Finally we have 3 {n C 3 = n(n-1)(n-2) / 3!}
 
 Also we have this "theoretical" way of writing it:
 {n C k = n! / ((n-k)! k!)}
 
 [;Pascals_rule
  It allows you to "decompose" by {n}
  {(n C k) + (n C k+1) = n+1 C k+1}
  or, if you wanna break down a larger choice
  {n C k = (n-1 C k-1) + (n-1 C k)}
  [#done How to remember why that's true?
   [Choosing {k=3} items out of {n=6} items out of the list
    {a b c d e f}
    Can be broken down into a sum of these two:
    1. The groups of 3 with {a} in them: there are {5 C 2} of them: {b c}, {b d} {e f}, etc.
    2. The groups of 3 without {a} in them: there are {5 C 3} of them {b c d}, {b d e}, etc.
    ]
   [NOTE It's still not at all easy to remember, but at least that's an interpretation]
   ]
  [#done Proof
   {(n C k) + (n C k+1)
    = (n! / ((n-k)!    k!) +
       n! / ((n-k-1)! (k+1)!))
    = (n! (k+1) / ((n-k)! (k+1)!) +
       n! (n-k) / ((n-k)! (k+1)!))
    = (n+1)! / (((n+1)-(k+1))! (k+1)!)
    = n+1 C k+1
   }]
  ]
 
 [#done Wait, how do we get {n C 0 = 1}?
  So if we follow @Pascals_rule, then
  {n C 1 = (n-1 C 0) + (n-1 C 1)
   -> n  = (n-1 C 0) + (n-1)
   -> n-1 C 0 = 1
  }]
 
 [#done What about {n C -1 = 0}?
  {n C 0 = (n-1 C -1) + (n-1 C 0)
   -> 1 = (n-1 C -1) + 1
   -> (n-1 C -1) = 0
  }]
 
 [What about {n C n+1 = 0}?
  {(n C n) + (n C n+1) = n+1 C n+1
   -> 1 + (n C n+1) = 1
   -> (n C n+1) = 0  // #qed
  }]
 
 [NOTE People don't realize,
  but there are a bunch of zeros outside of the Pascal triangle;
  |NOTE (You can add to the top, see the Mathematica page)
  | 0 0 1 0
  |0 0 1 1 0
  | 0 1 2 1 0
  |0 1 3 3 1 0
  ]
 
 [;binomial_shift
  We can decrement both {n} and {i} like this
  { binom(n,i) = n/i binom(n-1, i-1) }
  [Proof
   {
    i binom(n,i)
    = i n! / ((n-i)! i!)
    = n (n-1)! / (((n-1)-(i-1))! (i-1)!)
    = n binom(n-1, i-1)
   }
   ]
  ]
 ]

[;binomial_theorem
 {(a+b)^n = (n C 0) a^0 b^n + (n-1 C 1) a^1 b^(n-1) +...+ (n C n) a^n b^0}
 
 This can be proven by an informal argument:
 In general, {a^i} appears {n C i} times in the expansion, because
 {(a+b)^n = (a+b)(a+b)^(n-1)
  = a(a+b)^(n-1) + b(a+b)^(n-1)}
 
 {a^(i-1)} appears {n-1 C i-1} times in {(a+b)^(n-1)},
 and {a^i} appears {n-1 C i} times in {(a+b)^(n-1)}
 so in total, {a^i} appears {(n-1 C i-1) + (n-1 C i) = n C i} times
 
 And the entire expansion is just a collection of these, so #qed.
 ]

[;Bernstein_polynomial
 A bernstein polynomial is given by
 {n B i = (n C i) (1-t)^(n-i) t^i}
 
 [;bernstein_lerp They have this highly relevant property
  { lerp(n B i+1, t, n B i) = n+1 B i+1 }
  NOTE It even works when i=n, for example { t^4 = lerp(0, t, t^3) }
  (NOTE When we say {B}, we mean {B(t)})
  NOTE The ordering of the lerp arguments isn't intuitive!
  
  If you know @Pascals_rule, you know what's up
  {lerp(8 B 5, t, 8 B 4)
   = (1-t)(8 B 5) + t(8 B 4)
   = ((1-t) (8 C 5) (1-t)^3 t^5 +
      t     (8 C 4) (1-t)^4 t^4)
   = ((8 C 5) (1-t)^4 t^5 +
      (8 C 4) (1-t)^4 t^5)
   // NOTE The polynomials lined up!
   = (9 C 5) (1-t)^4 t^5
   = 9 B 5
  }
  ]
 
 [And with the @binomial_theorem, we can prove that
  all bernstein polynomials for degree {n} sum up to 1
  {n B 0 + n B 1 +...+ n B n = 1}
  [NOTE Of course it does, since {b(t)} is a linear combination of the control points
   per @Bezier_Bernstein, we need the coefficients to sum up to 1]]
 ]

[;Bezier_Bernstein
 Formula for Bezier curves expressed as an affine combination of the control points
 {b(t) = b[t times n] = sum(i in [0,n],
                            B__i^n(t) b__i)}
 
 [Intuitive elbow grease
  So we need to prove that, say
  {b2^3 = B0^3 b2 + B1^3 b3 + B2^3 b4 + B3^3 b5}
  
  [So let's just do it
   {b2^3
    = lerp(b2^2, t, b3^2)
    // Induction hypothesis
    = lerp(B0^2 b2 + B1^2 b3 + B2^2 b4,
           t,
           B0^2 b3 + B1^2 b4 + B2^2 b5)
    = lerp((1-t)^2 b2 + B1^2 b3 + B2^2 b4,
           t,
           B0^2 b3 + B1^2 b4 + t^2 b5)
    = (1-t)^3 b2 + lerp(B1^2 b3 + B2^2 b4, t, B0^2 b3 + B1^2 b4) + t^3 b5
    // Using @bernstein_lerp
    = B0^3 b2 + B1^2 b3 + B2^2 b4 + B3^3 b5  // #qed
   }
   // NOTE That wasn't a really good proof, even after I tried to make it shorter!
   ]
  ]
 
 [NOTE This is a corollary of @Leibniz_formula]
 ]

[;Leibniz_formula Suppose {alpha+beta = 1}, we wanna prove that
 {
  b[(alpha r + beta s) times n]
  = sum(i in [0,n],
        (n C i) alpha^i beta^(n-i) b[r times i, s times n-i])
 }
 [NOTE This is basically @bezier_subdivision]
 
 Suppose {n=3}
 {
  b[(alpha r + beta s) times 3] =
  (1 alpha^0 beta^3 b[s,s,s] +
   3 alpha^1 beta^2 b[r,s,s] +
   3 alpha^2 beta^1 b[r,r,s] +
   1 alpha^3 beta^0 b[r,r,r])
 }
 
 [Proof
  The base case: {n=1}
  {
   b[(alpha r + beta s)]
   = ((n C 0) alpha^0 beta^1 b[s] +
      (n C 1) alpha^1 beta^0 b[r])
   = beta b[s] + alpha b[r]
   // which is true, so #qed
  }
  
  NOTE We can decrease blossom degree like so (it's just a normal de Casteljau step)
  {
   b[t,t,t]
   = ([b[t,0,0], b[t,0,1], b[t,1,1]])[t,t] = b[t][t,t]
   = (b[t,t,0], b[t,t,1])[t]               = b[t,t][t] 
  }
  
  The inductive case
  {
   b[(alpha r + beta s) times n+1]
   = b[alpha r + beta s][(alpha r + beta s) times n]
   // Inductive hypothesis
   = sum(i in [0,n],
         (n C i) alpha^i beta^(n-i) b[alpha r + beta s, r times i, s times n-i])
   // Using @blossom_multiaffine
   = sum(i in [0,n],
         (n C i) alpha^(i+1) beta^(n-i)   b[r times i+1, s times n-i] +
         (n C i) alpha^i     beta^(n-i+1) b[r times i, s times n-i+1]
         )
   [// NOTE Let's remind ourselves of the goal
    sum(i in [0,n+1],
        (n+1 C i) alpha^(i) beta^(n+1-i) b[r times i, s times n+1-i])
    ]
   // Split the sum out, adjust the first sum index
   = (sum(i in [1,n+1],
          (n C i-1) alpha^i beta^(n-i+1) b[r times i, s times n-i+1]) +
      sum(i in [0,n],
          (n C i) alpha^i beta^(n-i+1) b[r times i, s times n-i+1]))
   // Tricky: We can let the indices run out-of-range (so that they match)
   // all the expressions make sense, and the coefficients are zero (see @binomial_coefficients).
   = (sum(i in [0,n+1],
          (n C i-1) alpha^i beta^(n-i+1) b[r times i, s times n-i+1]) +
      sum(i in [0,n+1],
          (n C i) alpha^i beta^(n-i+1) b[r times i, s times n-i+1]))
   // Combine with @Pascals_rule!
   = sum(i in [0,n+1],
         (n+1 C i) alpha^i beta^(n+1-i) b[r times i, s times n+1-i])
   // #qed
  }
  ]
 ]

[Can you compute the intermediate control points
 as an affine combination of the (original) control points?
 {b__i^r(t) = sum(j in [0,r],
                  B__j^r(t) b__(j+i))}
 Of course, you're just doing the deCasteljau process on the slice {b[i:r+1]}
 ]

[Proof that {lerp} and {unlerp} on the same interval are inverse 
 { f(u) = unlerp(a,u,b) = (u-a)/(b-a) }
 { x(u) = lerp(a,u,b)   = (1-u)a + u b }
 
 Then we have { f(x(u)) = x(f(u)) }
 
 [Proof: I mean those are inverses because we can do algebra like this
  {(e1)
   t = f(u)
   <-> t = (u-a)/(b-a)
   <-> u = a + (b-a)t
   <-> u = x(t)
  }
  
  [NOTE The rest is generic to content of {f} and {x}
   [Proof that { f(x(t)) == t }
    If we set { u = x(t) }, due to {e1}, { t = f(u) }, so
    { t = f(u) = f(x(t)) }]
   
   [Proof that { x(f(u)) == u } is the same deal!]]
  ]
 ]

[Symmetry of Bezier curves
 If we reverse the order of the control points,
 obtaining { c = reverse(b) } what happens? 
 
 [NOTE Lemma: { B__i^n(1-t) == B__(n-i)^n(t) }]
 We can prove { b(t) = c(1-t) }
 {
  c(1-t)
  = sum(i in [0,n],  B__i^n(1-t) c__i)
  = sum(i in [0,n],  B__(n-i)^n(t) c__(n-i))
  [Set j := n-i]
  = sum(j in [0,n],  B__j^n(t) c__j)
  = b(t)
 }
 ]

[Invariance under barycentric combination
 The weighted average (lerp) two of Bezier curves is another Bezier curve
 {
  (alpha b + beta c)(t)
  = sum(i in [0,n],
        B__i^n (alpha b__i + beta c__i))
  = alpha b(t) + beta c(t)
 }
 ]

[Linear precision
 [Lemma: { sum(i in [0,n],  i B__i^n(t)) = n t }
  Don't believe me? Try @linear_precision_identity_test
  
  [Proof... thanks to Grok, and the source that it ~~stole from~~ trained on
   {
    sum(i in [0,n],  i/n B__i^n(t))
    = sum(i in [0,n],  i/n binom(n,i) (1-t)^(n-i) t^i)
    // NOTE The term at i=0 doesn't do anything, so we can ignore it
    = sum(i in [1,n],  i/n binom(n,i) (1-t)^(n-i) t^i)
    // due to @binomial_shift
    = sum(i in [1,n],  binom(n-1, i-1) (1-t)^(n-i) t^i)
    // change i to i+1
    = sum(i in [0,n-1],  binom(n-1, i) (1-t)^(n-1-i) t^(i+1))
    = t sum(i in [0,n-1],  B__i^(n-1))
    //... this is incredible!
    = t
   }
   ]
  
  [A more straight-forward proof (source: me)
   {
    sum(i in [0,n],  i B__i^n(t))
    // using @bernstein_lerp (the i=0 case doesn't matter)
    = sum(i in [1,n],  i lerp(B__i^(n-1)(t),
                              t,
                              B__(i-1)^(n-1)(t)))
    = ((1-t) sum(i in [1,n], i B__i    ^(n-1)(t)) +
       t     sum(i in [1,n], i B__(i-1)^(n-1)(t)))
    {// Detour on the first term
     sum(i in [1,n], i B__i^(n-1)(t))
     = sum(i in [1,n-1], i B__i^(n-1)(t)) + n B__n^(n-1)(t)
     // second term is 0 :>, also i can run from 0 for the first term
     = sum(i in [0,n-1], i B__i^(n-1)(t))
     // using induction hypothesis
     = (n-1) t
    }
    {// Detour on the second term!
     sum(i in [1,n],  i B__(i-1)^(n-1)(t))
     // change i to i+1
     = sum(i in [0,n-1], (i+1) B__i^(n-1)(t))
     // don't be afraid of the "i+1"
     = sum(i in [0,n-1], (i B__i^(n-1)(t) +
                          B__i^(n-1)(t)))
     // using partition of unity, and induction hypothesis
     = 1 + (n-1) t
    }
    // NOTE coming back
    = (1-t) (n-1) t + t (1+(n-1)t)
    = lerp((n-1) t, t, 1+(n-1)t)
    = (n-1) t + t
    = n t
   }
   ]
  ]
 
 [Suppose the control points {b__i} are uniformly distributed on the line segment {p,q}
  { b__i = (1-i/n)p + i/n q; i in [0,n] }
  Then what is the output?
  {
   b(t)
   = sum(i in [0,n], B__i^n(t) b__i)
   = sum(i in [0,n], B__i^n(t) ((1-i/n)p + i/n q))
   // Using the insane identity above...
   = (1-t)p + t q
  }
  ]
 ]

